items.bodyMarkdown:
EF Core gives you one transaction for free, and it is drawn in a specific place: around a **single call to `SaveChanges`**.

Everything tracked as added, modified or deleted at the moment you call it is written inside one transaction. Twenty entities, five tables, does not matter — either every statement commits or none does. That is a genuinely useful default, and it means the common case (build up a change, save it once) is atomic without you asking.

The trap is the plural. Two calls to `SaveChanges` are two transactions, with a gap between them in which anything can happen: an exception, a timeout, a deployment, the machine dying. If the second call is what makes the first one *correct*, that gap is a partial write waiting to be observed.

```csharp
order.Status = "submitted";
await db.SaveChangesAsync();                 // transaction 1 — committed

db.StockMovements.Add(new StockMovement(...));
await db.SaveChangesAsync();                 // transaction 2 — may never run
```

The order is submitted and the stock never moved. Nothing here throws; the code is simply describing two independent facts to the database when it meant to describe one.

The fix is usually to save once. Add everything to the context, then call `SaveChanges` a single time — EF Core works out the statement order from the relationships, and the whole thing is atomic.

When one save genuinely will not do — because you need to read something *back* mid-way, or call a stored procedure, or the work spans two contexts — take the transaction explicitly:

```csharp
await using var tx = await db.Database.BeginTransactionAsync();
try
{
    // ... work, possibly several SaveChanges calls ...
    await tx.CommitAsync();
}
catch
{
    await tx.RollbackAsync();   // or rely on dispose, which rolls back
    throw;
}
```

Now the boundary is where you drew it rather than where the framework happened to put it.

Two cautions that matter more in .NET than they might seem.

**A transaction is not a lock you can hold casually.** Everything inside it holds its locks until commit, so a transaction that spans an HTTP call to a payment provider can block other writers for as long as that provider is slow. Keep external calls outside the boundary.

**Rolling back the database does not roll back your objects.** After a failed save the context still holds the modified entities, in their modified state, still tracked. Reusing that context afterwards will try to save them again. The safe pattern is a fresh context per unit of work — which is what the default scoped lifetime in ASP.NET Core gives you, one per request.

items.workedExampleMarkdown:
Larkspur closes a ticket and writes an audit row. Two versions.

**Version A — two saves:**

```csharp
var ticket = await db.Tickets.FindAsync(id);
ticket!.Status = "closed";
ticket.ClosedAt = clock.UtcNow;
await db.SaveChangesAsync();                     // commit 1

db.AuditEntries.Add(new AuditEntry(id, "closed", actorId));
await db.SaveChangesAsync();                     // commit 2
```

If the second save fails — a constraint, a dropped connection, a redeploy — the ticket is closed and there is no record of who closed it. The audit table's whole purpose is to be complete, and this code makes completeness best-effort.

**Version B — one save:**

```csharp
var ticket = await db.Tickets.FindAsync(id);
ticket!.Status = "closed";
ticket.ClosedAt = clock.UtcNow;
db.AuditEntries.Add(new AuditEntry(id, "closed", actorId));

await db.SaveChangesAsync();                     // one transaction, both rows
```

One transaction. The UPDATE and the INSERT commit together or not at all, and the audit table cannot disagree with the ticket table.

Now a case where one save is not enough — the order number must be read back from a sequence and then used:

```csharp
await using var tx = await db.Database.BeginTransactionAsync();

var order = new Order { CustomerId = customerId, Status = "draft" };
db.Orders.Add(order);
await db.SaveChangesAsync();                     // order.Id is now populated

order.Reference = $"ORD-{order.Id:D6}";
db.OrderEvents.Add(new OrderEvent(order.Id, "created"));
await db.SaveChangesAsync();

await tx.CommitAsync();                          // both saves commit together
```

Two `SaveChanges` calls, one transaction. Neither save is visible to anyone else until `CommitAsync`, so a failure anywhere leaves no order at all — which is the correct outcome, because an order with no reference is not an order.

items.promptMarkdown:
Larkspur's inventory service reserves stock when an order is submitted.

```csharp
var order = await db.Orders.FindAsync(orderId);
order!.Status = "submitted";
await db.SaveChangesAsync();

foreach (var line in order.Lines)
{
    var stock = await db.Stock.FindAsync(line.ProductId);
    stock!.Reserved += line.Quantity;
}
await db.SaveChangesAsync();
```

The second save occasionally fails with a check-constraint violation when stock would go negative. What state can the database be left in, and what is the minimal fix?

solution.code:
function rollbackScope(steps, failedAt) {
  const index = steps.findIndex(s => s.id === failedAt);
  if (index === -1) return [];
  const before = steps.slice(0, index);
  const writes = before.filter(s => s.kind === 'write');
  return writes.map(s => s.id);
}

